Cycles: a native VSX CPU kernel for powerpc64le, plus the SSE kernel through
the compiler's SSE-on-VSX compatibility headers as a second option.

Cycles has one 4-wide CPU kernel, written against SSE intrinsics and selected
by __KERNEL_SSE__..__KERNEL_SSE42__ (util/optimization.h); ARM compiles the
same code through sse2neon.  On POWER nothing set those macros, so every
kernel was scalar.  This adds, selectable with CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD:

  VSX      (default) the vector layer written on VSX itself: float4/float3
           hold a `__vector float`, int4/int3 a `__vector int`, and every
           operation the kernel needs -- arithmetic, compares, select,
           shuffles, reductions, dot, cross, floor/ceil/trunc/round, int<->
           float conversion, transforms, the ray/triangle helpers -- has a
           vec_* / GCC-vector-extension implementation under __KERNEL_VSX__
           in the util headers.  No x86 intrinsic is used or emulated; the
           build itself proves it, since no compatibility header is included
           and any surviving _mm_* would fail to compile.
  SSE2VSX  the x86 kernel unchanged, through GCC's/clang's <xmmintrin.h> ..
           <nmmintrin.h> for powerpc64le (__KERNEL_SSE2VSX__), with the one
           intrinsic they lack (_mm_dp_ps) supplied in simd.h.
  SCALAR   no SIMD kernel.

Both SIMD modes define the __KERNEL_SSE*__ macros, because that is what the
kernel tests for "a 4-wide kernel exists" (svm/noise.h, hash.h, color.h,
transform.h ...); those algorithm sites are ISA-neutral and use only the
float4/int4 API.

Design of the native layer, each choice checked on the disassembly
(GCC 16.1.1, -O2 -mcpu=power9, ISA 3.0):

- shuffles go through __builtin_shuffle with a constant mask so the compiler
  emits the single-op permute where one exists (xxswapd, xxmrghd/xxmrgld,
  xxspltw, xxmrghw/xxmrglw) and xxperm with a loaded control vector only
  otherwise; the horizontal reductions (reduce_add/min/max, dot) rotate with
  vec_sld(a, a, 12|8), one vsldoi each and no control vector at all;
- madd/msub are vec_madd/vec_msub: with Cycles' -ffp-contract=on GCC does not
  fuse a * b + c written with the vector operators (it does fuse the scalar
  kernel's expressions), so the compat path had 6 fused vector ops and this
  one has 2,194;
- float3 reductions zero the w lane with vec_insert(0, a, 3) -- xxspltib +
  xxinsertw, no memory constant -- and dot() sums (a0+a1)+(a2+a3), the
  association the scalar float4 dot uses;
- transform_point/direction transpose the three rows with vec_mergeh/mergel
  + xxmrghd/xxmrgld (8 ops) and apply three vec_madd of splatted components;
- float->int conversion truncates (vctsxs, saturating), matching the scalar
  (int) casts rather than SSE's round-to-nearest cvtps;
- neg/abs/sqrt/floor/ceil/trunc are the single VSX instructions (xvnegsp,
  xvabssp, xvsqrtsp, xvrspim/ip/iz); round is vrfin, ties to even like
  _mm_round_ps(NINT) (scalar roundf rounds ties away); min/max are
  xvminsp/xvmaxsp, which return the non-NaN operand (IEEE minNum) where the
  scalar (a < b) ? a : b returns b -- both documented differences;
- bit scans use __builtin_ctz/clz on any GCC/clang target instead of the
  32/64-iteration loops.

Kernel object (kernel/device/cpu/kernel.cpp, the whole integrator), same
flags, scalar / SSE2VSX / VSX: instructions 296,749 / 336,909 / 303,769;
xxperm+vperm 0 / 10,675 / 895; fused vector multiply-adds 0 / 6 / 2,194;
lxv 1,578 / 11,602 / 8,064; all three use all 64 VSRs.

Verified on POWER9: a 8,967-operation probe against the scalar definitions
(handbook probes/cycles_vsx_probe.cpp) -- 8,276 bit-identical, the rest
within rounding-order tolerance, 3 documented differences, 0 mismatches;
BMW27 and Classroom render to PSNR 59 / 49 dB against the scalar kernel,
the same as the SSE kernel does.

Portability work, worth sending upstream (projects.blender.org, Cycles).
No POWER kernel exists anywhere else.

diff -ruN -x obj a/intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt b/intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt	2026-02-09 09:34:40.000000000 -0700
+++ b/intern/cycles/CMakeLists.txt	2026-09-11 00:06:28.144285492 -0600
@@ -30,6 +30,45 @@
 # TODO: this code could be refactored a bit to avoid duplication.
 # NOTE: CXX_HAS_SSE42 is needed in case passing SSE flags fails altogether (`gcc-arm`).
 
+# powerpc64le: which CPU kernel to build.
+#   VSX      the 4-wide kernel written natively on VSX (util/simd.h, __KERNEL_VSX__)
+#   SSE2VSX  the x86 SSE4.2 kernel compiled through the compiler's SSE-on-VSX
+#            compatibility headers (GCC >= 8, clang; __KERNEL_SSE2VSX__)
+#   SCALAR   no SIMD kernel
+if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES "^(ppc64le|powerpc64le)$")
+  set(CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD "VSX" CACHE STRING
+      "Cycles CPU kernel SIMD on ppc64le: VSX (native), SSE2VSX (SSE kernel via compat headers) or SCALAR")
+  set_property(CACHE CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD PROPERTY STRINGS VSX SSE2VSX SCALAR)
+  include(CheckCXXSourceCompiles)
+  if(CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD STREQUAL "VSX")
+    check_cxx_source_compiles(
+      "#include <altivec.h>
+       int main() {
+         __vector float a = {1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f};
+         a = vec_madd(a, a, a);
+         return vec_extract(a, 0) == 2.0f ? 0 : 1;
+       }"
+      SUPPORTS_VSX_BUILD)
+    if(NOT SUPPORTS_VSX_BUILD)
+      message(FATAL_ERROR "CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD=VSX but <altivec.h>/VSX is not available (need -mcpu=power8 or later)")
+    endif()
+  elseif(CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD STREQUAL "SSE2VSX")
+    check_cxx_source_compiles(
+      "#define NO_WARN_X86_INTRINSICS 1
+       #include <nmmintrin.h>
+       int main() {
+         __m128 a = _mm_set1_ps(1.0f);
+         return _mm_movemask_ps(_mm_cmpeq_ps(a, a)) == 0xf ? 0 : 1;
+       }"
+      SUPPORTS_SSE2VSX_BUILD)
+    if(NOT SUPPORTS_SSE2VSX_BUILD)
+      message(FATAL_ERROR "CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD=SSE2VSX but the compiler has no SSE-on-VSX compatibility headers")
+    endif()
+  elseif(NOT CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD STREQUAL "SCALAR")
+    message(FATAL_ERROR "CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD must be VSX, SSE2VSX or SCALAR")
+  endif()
+endif()
+
 # Determine vectorization support and required build flags
 if(WITH_CYCLES_NATIVE_ONLY)
   set(CXX_HAS_SSE42 FALSE)
@@ -73,6 +112,17 @@
   set(CXX_HAS_SSE42 FALSE)
   set(CXX_HAS_AVX2 FALSE)
   set(CXX_HAS_F16C FALSE)
+elseif(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES "^(ppc64le|powerpc64le)$" AND NOT CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD STREQUAL "SCALAR")
+  # POWER: no SSE or AVX compiler flags exist; util/optimization.h selects the
+  # kernel on WITH_VSX_NATIVE / WITH_SSE2VSX.
+  set(CXX_HAS_SSE42 FALSE)
+  set(CXX_HAS_AVX2 FALSE)
+  set(CXX_HAS_F16C FALSE)
+  if(CYCLES_PPC64LE_SIMD STREQUAL "VSX")
+    add_definitions(-DWITH_VSX_NATIVE)
+  else()
+    add_definitions(-DWITH_SSE2VSX -DNO_WARN_X86_INTRINSICS)
+  endif()
 elseif(WIN32 AND MSVC AND NOT CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES "Clang")
   set(CXX_HAS_SSE42 TRUE)
   set(CXX_HAS_AVX2 TRUE)
diff -ruN -x obj a/intern/cycles/device/cpu/device.cpp b/intern/cycles/device/cpu/device.cpp
--- a/intern/cycles/device/cpu/device.cpp	2026-02-08 22:15:57.000000000 -0700
+++ b/intern/cycles/device/cpu/device.cpp	2026-09-11 00:06:28.145460533 -0600
@@ -48,7 +48,15 @@
 
 string device_cpu_capabilities()
 {
+#if defined(__KERNEL_VSX__)
+  /* One kernel, native VSX; nothing to detect at runtime. */
+  return "VSX";
+#elif defined(__KERNEL_SSE2VSX__)
+  /* One kernel, the SSE4.2 one through the compiler's SSE-on-VSX headers. */
+  return "SSE4.2 on VSX";
+#else
   return system_cpu_support_avx2() ? "AVX2" : "";
+#endif
 }
 
 CCL_NAMESPACE_END
